## 2. Project Location & Context

### 2.1 Location

The Itombwe Nature Reserve is located in the South Kivu Province of the Democratic Republic of Congo. It forms part of the Albertine Rift, one of Africa's most biodiverse regions.

- **Province:** South Kivu
- **Country:** Democratic Republic of Congo
- **Ecoregion:** Albertine Rift montane forests

### 2.2 The Itombwe Massif

The Itombwe massif is a highland region in the eastern part of the DRC, extending west of Lake Tanganyika. It comprises a series of high plateaus and mountains of the western branch of the East African Rift. The massif has about 20 peaks over 2,500 metres, with the highest summit at 3,275 metres.

The massif is drained by several tributaries of the Congo River, including:
- Lowa River (draining north)
- Elila River (draining west)
- Ulindi River (draining west)
- Mulambozi River

### 2.3 Protection Status

The Itombwe Nature Reserve was established by ministerial decree in 2006. It is managed by the Institut Congolais pour la Conservation de la Nature (ICCN). The reserve is recognised as an Important Bird Area (IBA) and is a candidate site for World Heritage status.

