## 3. Biodiversity Significance

### 3.1 Key Species

The Itombwe massif harbours exceptional biodiversity, including:

| **Species** | **Scientific Name** | **Conservation Status** |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Grauer's gorilla** | *Gorilla beringei graueri* | Critically Endangered (IUCN) |
| **Eastern chimpanzee** | *Pan troglodytes schweinfurthii* | Endangered (IUCN) |
| **Forest elephant** | *Loxodonta cyclotis* | Critically Endangered (IUCN) |
| **Various primate species** | Multiple | Various |

The Grauer's gorilla is the world's largest primate. It is endemic to the eastern DRC and is one of the two subspecies of eastern gorilla. The Itombwe massif harbours one of the last viable populations of this species.

### 3.2 Additional Biodiversity

The Itombwe massif also supports:
- Significant populations of other mammal species
- Rich avian diversity (Important Bird Area designation)
- Exceptional plant biodiversity
- High endemism typical of the Albertine Rift

### 3.3 Ecosystem Significance

The Itombwe massif's ecosystems provide critical ecosystem services, including:
- Carbon storage
- Water catchment and regulation
- Soil conservation
- Maintenance of biodiversity
- Climate regulation

